/// Chapter 36 of the guide, written from md-docs/recipes/desktop-apps.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page DesktopGuide(path: "/docs/guide/desktop", title: "Desktop apps", description: "Make a WebFluent project a Tauri or Electron desktop app: set it up, run it while you work, call Rust or Node from a page, hear events, and package it.", layout: DocsShell("Desktop apps", chapter: t("nav.group.ship"), blurb: "Turn a WebFluent project into a Tauri or Electron desktop app, call the native side from a page, and package it.", source: "md-docs/recipes/desktop-apps.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "prepare-the-build-for-a-desktop", label: "Prepare the build for a desktop"), Anchor(id: "make-a-tauri-app", label: "Make a Tauri app"), Anchor(id: "make-an-electron-app", label: "Make an Electron app"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/environments", prevLabel: t("ch.35"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/pdf-and-slides", nextLabel: t("ch.37"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to ship a WebFluent project as a desktop app with Tauri or Electron, and how a page asks the native side for what a browser cannot do: read a file on disk, open a folder dialog, show a notification, start a process.\n\nA *desktop shell* is a window that shows a web page, plus a native side that the page can call. `wf build` writes a folder of static files ([Deploying](/docs/guide/deploying)), and that folder is what the shell shows. Nothing in the language changes for a desktop app. A page reaches the native side through a plain script under `src/` ([JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop)), with one typed function per thing it asks for, and the shell decides what that script can reach.\n\nChoose a shell by the language you want on the native side:")

        Table(caption: "Desktop apps") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("").header
                    Table.Cell("Tauri").header
                    Table.Cell("Electron").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The native side is written in")
                    Table.Cell("Rust")
                    Table.Cell("JavaScript, on Node.js")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The window is")
                    Table.Cell("the system's own web view")
                    Table.Cell("a Chromium shipped inside the app")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A page reaches the native side through")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`window.__TAURI__`") }
                    Table.Cell("what your preload script exposes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You need")
                    Table.Cell("Rust and the Tauri CLI")
                    Table.Cell("Node.js")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Both run the same pages, the same runtime and the same security policy.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You need:\n\n- a WebFluent project that builds with `wf build`;\n- for Tauri: Rust, and the system libraries listed in Tauri's [prerequisites](https://v2.tauri.app/start/prerequisites/);\n- for Electron: Node.js and npm.")

        Heading("Prepare the build for a desktop", id: "prepare-the-build-for-a-desktop").h2

        Markdown("The same project builds for the web and for a desktop. To leave out what only a website needs, set two keys in `webfluent.app.json`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
    "build": { "compress": false },
    "meta": { "sitemap": false }
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("- **`build.compress: false`** — the shell packs the folder into the app, so a `.gz` file beside every file would ship every file twice.\n- **`meta.sitemap: false`** — writes no `sitemap.xml` or `robots.txt`; nothing crawls an app. You can leave `meta.site_url` and the link-preview tags unset too.\n- **Leave `offline` out** — the files are already on the machine, so a service worker has nothing to keep.\n\nA single-page build and a static build both work ([Static sites and SPAs](/docs/guide/static-and-spa)), and addresses stay as they are. Both shells in this chapter answer a path the build has no file for with `index.html`, so reloading the window on `/settings` shows the settings page.\n\nAn app is often used with no network, and a font named in `meta.fonts` comes from another origin. To make a font always available, put the file in `public/` and declare it with `@font-face` in a `.css` file under `src/` ([Styling](/docs/guide/styling)).")

        Heading("Make a Tauri app", id: "make-a-tauri-app").h2

        Markdown("Tauri runs your build in the system's web view and gives the page a Rust side to call.")

        Heading("Set up Tauri", id: "set-up-tauri").h3

        Markdown("1. Install the Tauri CLI once:\n\n   ```bash cargo install tauri-cli --version \"^2\" --locked ```\n\n2. In the project's folder, create the Rust side:\n\n   ```bash cargo tauri init --app-name notes --window-title Notes \\ --frontend-dist ../build --dev-url http://localhost:3000 \\ --before-dev-command \"wf serve\" --before-build-command \"wf build\" ```\n\n   This writes `src-tauri/` beside `src/`: the Rust side, its `Cargo.toml` and `tauri.conf.json`. WebFluent never reads `src-tauri/`, and Tauri runs the two `wf` commands in the project's folder.\n\n3. Change two keys in `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`:\n\n   ```json \{ \"identifier\": \"com.example.notes\", \"app\": \{ \"withGlobalTauri\": true \} \} ```\n\n   - **`identifier`** — a reverse-domain name of your own. `cargo tauri build` refuses the default, `com.tauri.dev`.\n   - **`withGlobalTauri`** — puts Tauri's JavaScript API on `window.__TAURI__`, every plugin's API included, so a plain script uses it with no `import` and no npm.\n\nLeave `app.security.csp` at `null`: every page already carries its own policy (see [Allow Tauri requests in the security policy](#allow-tauri-requests-in-the-security-policy)).")

        Heading("Run the Tauri app while you work", id: "run-the-tauri-app-while-you-work").h3

        Markdown("To work on the app, run:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo tauri dev")
            ])

        Markdown("This command runs `wf serve` and opens the app's window on it. Saving a `.wf` file rebuilds and reloads the window, and a build that fails shows its error over the page, as it does in a browser ([CLI](/docs/guide/cli)). Saving a Rust file rebuilds the app. If `dev.port` is not `3000`, change `devUrl` to match.\n\nTo build the installers, run:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo tauri build")
            ])

        Markdown("This command runs `wf build`, embeds `build/` in the binary, and writes the installers under `src-tauri/target/release/bundle/`.")

        Heading("Call a Rust command", id: "call-a-rust-command").h3

        Markdown("A *command* is a Rust function a page may call. Write it in `src-tauri/src/lib.rs` and list it in `run()`. This file has two commands: `greet` returns a value, and `start_sync` returns at once and reports its progress as events:")

        CodeBlock(#"use tauri::Emitter;

#[tauri::command]
fn greet(name: &str) -> String {
    format!("Hello, {name}!")
}

#[tauri::command]
fn start_sync(app: tauri::AppHandle) {
    std::thread::spawn(move || {
        for done in [25, 50, 75, 100] {
            // … a quarter of the work …
            let _ = app.emit("sync-progress", done);
        }
    });
}

// in run(), before .run(…):
//     .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![greet, start_sync])"#, title: "rust")

        Markdown("A page calls a command through a script that names it. Write one function per command, typed with JSDoc, so `.wf` code calls `greet(name)` like any other function and the checker holds every call to its types:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/native.js
/**
 * Whether the page is inside the app, rather than a browser tab.
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function inApp() {
  return "__TAURI__" in window;
}

/**
 * Call a Rust command. What its `Err` holds arrives as an `Error`.
 * @param {string} command
 * @param {object} [args]
 * @returns {Promise<any>}
 */
function invoke(command, args) {
  if (!inApp()) return Promise.reject(new Error("This needs the desktop app."));
  return window.__TAURI__.core.invoke(command, args).catch((err) => {
    throw new Error(typeof err === "string" ? err : JSON.stringify(err));
  });
}

/**
 * @param {string} name
 * @returns {Promise<string>}
 */
function greet(name) {
  return invoke("greet", { name });
}

/** Start syncing; how far it has got arrives as `sync-progress`. */
function startSync() {
  return invoke("start_sync");
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("Three rules hold for every command:\n\n- **Arguments are named.** A command's parameters are the keys of the object, in camelCase: a Rust `note_id` is `\{ noteId \}`.\n- **A result is a promise.** A command that returns `Result<T, E>` resolves with the `T`. Its `Err` rejects, and `invoke` turns it into an `Error`, so a page's `catch e` reads `e.message`.\n- **`inApp()` is false in a browser tab** — `wf serve` opened in a browser, `wf test`, `wf verify` — where there is no native side. Each function answers there instead of throwing, so every page still loads.\n\nThe page awaits the function in an action and shows the reply:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Hello(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "Says hello from Rust.") {
    state name = ""
    state reply = ""

    action ask() {
        try {
            reply = await greet(name)
        } catch e {
            reply = e.message
        }
    }

    Heading("Hello").h1
    if !inApp() {
        Alert("Open this in the app to talk to Rust.").info
    }
    Input(bind: name, label: "Your name")
    Button("Greet").primary { on click { ask() } }
    Text(reply)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("In the app, typing `Ada` and pressing **Greet** shows `Hello, Ada!`. In a browser tab, the page shows the alert, and **Greet** shows `This needs the desktop app.`")

        Heading("Listen for events from Rust", id: "listen-for-events-from-rust").h3

        Markdown("`start_sync` returns at once and reports as it goes, with `app.emit(…)`. A page hears an event for as long as it shows, so tie the listener to the page's lifetime: `mount:` starts it, and `cleanup:` stops it when the page, branch or item leaves. First, wrap Tauri's `listen` in a script:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/events.js
/**
 * Hear an event the native side emits, until the function it returns is called.
 * @param {string} name
 * @param {(payload: any) => void} handler
 * @returns {() => void}
 */
function onEvent(name, handler) {
  if (!inApp()) return () => {};
  const listening = window.__TAURI__.event.listen(name, (e) => handler(e.payload));
  return () => { listening.then((stop) => stop()); };
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("Then hand the listener an action. This page shows a progress bar that fills as the events arrive:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Sync(path: "/sync", title: "Sync", description: "How far the sync has got.") {
    state done = 0
    state problem = ""

    action setDone(n: Number) { done = n }
    action start() {
        try {
            await startSync()
        } catch e {
            problem = e.message
        }
    }

    Stack(gap: .md,
          mount: (n) => onEvent("sync-progress", setDone),
          cleanup: (stop) => stop()) {
        Heading("Sync").h1
        Progress(value: done, max: 100)
        Text("{done}%")
        Button("Sync now") { on click { start() } }
        Text(problem)
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Pressing **Sync now** moves the bar through 25, 50, 75 and 100%.")

        Heading("Use a Tauri plugin", id: "use-a-tauri-plugin").h3

        Markdown("A dialog, the file system, notifications, the clipboard, opening a link in the browser: each is a Tauri plugin. To add one, run the CLI. It adds the crate, the line in `run()`, and the plugin's default permission in `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo tauri add dialog")
            ])

        Markdown("With `withGlobalTauri` on, the plugin's API is on `window.__TAURI__.dialog`. Wrap what a page uses, as with a command:")

        CodeBlock(#"// src/dialogs.js
/**
 * Ask for a folder; `null` when the reader cancels.
 * @returns {Promise<?string>}
 */
function pickFolder() {
  if (!inApp()) return Promise.resolve(null);
  return window.__TAURI__.dialog.open({ directory: true });
}"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("This page asks for a folder and shows the one the reader picked:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Library(path: "/library", title: "Library", description: "Where the notes are kept.") {
    state folder = ""

    action choose() {
        let picked = await pickFolder()
        if picked != null {
            folder = picked
        }
    }

    Heading("Library").h1
    Button("Choose a folder") { on click { choose() } }
    if folder != "" {
        Text("Notes are kept in {folder}.")
    } else {
        Text("No folder yet.").muted
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A plugin's call fails until its permission is granted. `cargo tauri add` grants the default set. Name anything beyond it — for example, a path the `fs` plugin may read — in the same capabilities file.")

        Heading("Allow Tauri requests in the security policy", id: "allow-tauri-requests-in-the-security-policy").h3

        Markdown("With `build.csp` on — as it is for a project `wf init` makes — every page says where it may send requests ([Security](/docs/guide/security)). Tauri sends each call as a request: to `http://ipc.localhost` on Windows and Android, and to `ipc://localhost` on macOS, Linux and iOS. Name both in `meta.connect`, the second as its scheme alone, which is how a policy names an app's own scheme:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "meta": { "connect": ["ipc:", "http://ipc.localhost"] } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("Other settings follow the same pattern:\n\n- **A protocol of your own** (`register_uri_scheme_protocol`) is named the same way. For a frame the app fills from it, write `\"frame\": [\"myapp:\", \"http://myapp.localhost\"]`; a frame's `src` may then use that scheme.\n- **WebAssembly**: a page that compiles it adds `\"wasm\": true`.\n- **Style elements**: a page whose scripts write `<style>` elements adds `\"style_elements\": true`.")

        Heading("Make an Electron app", id: "make-an-electron-app").h2

        Markdown("Electron ships its own Chromium and gives the page a Node.js side to call.")

        Heading("Set up Electron", id: "set-up-electron").h3

        Markdown("1. In the project's folder, install Electron:\n\n   ```bash npm init -y npm install --save-dev electron ```\n\n2. Create a folder called `electron/` for Electron's two files. Do **not** put them under `src/`: every `.js` file under `src/` is linked into the pages, and these files run in Node, outside the pages.\n\n3. Point `package.json` at the main process:\n\n   ```json \{ \"main\": \"electron/main.js\", \"scripts\": \{ \"start\": \"electron .\" \} \} ```\n\n4. Write the main process. It opens the window and serves the build to it. A `file://` address would break the build's absolute addresses (there, `/app.js` is the root of the disk), so the build is served from an `app://` origin of its own:")

        CodeBlock(#"// electron/main.js
const { app, BrowserWindow, dialog, ipcMain, net, protocol } = require("electron");
const fs = require("node:fs");
const path = require("node:path");
const { pathToFileURL } = require("node:url");

const BUILD = path.join(__dirname, "..", "build");

// To the page, app://localhost is an ordinary origin: absolute paths,
// fetch and its security policy work as they do on a site.
protocol.registerSchemesAsPrivileged([
  { scheme: "app", privileges: { standard: true, secure: true, supportFetchAPI: true } },
]);

const isFile = (p) => fs.statSync(p, { throwIfNoEntry: false })?.isFile() ?? false;

// A file of the build; a route the router draws gets index.html.
function serve(request) {
  const file = path.join(BUILD, decodeURIComponent(new URL(request.url).pathname));
  const found =
    file.startsWith(BUILD + path.sep) &&
    ([file, path.join(file, "index.html")].find(isFile) ??
      (path.extname(file) ? null : path.join(BUILD, "index.html")));
  return found
    ? net.fetch(pathToFileURL(found).href)
    : new Response("Not found", { status: 404 });
}

function openWindow() {
  const win = new BrowserWindow({
    width: 1000,
    height: 700,
    webPreferences: { preload: path.join(__dirname, "preload.js") },
  });
  // Packaged, the window shows the build; while you work, `wf serve`.
  win.loadURL(app.isPackaged ? "app://localhost/" : "http://localhost:3000/");
}

app.whenReady().then(() => {
  protocol.handle("app", serve);

  ipcMain.handle("greet", (_event, name) => `Hello, ${name}!`);
  ipcMain.handle("start-sync", (event) => {
    for (const done of [25, 50, 75, 100]) {
      // … a quarter of the work …
      event.sender.send("sync-progress", done);
    }
  });
  ipcMain.handle("pick-folder", async (event) => {
    const win = BrowserWindow.fromWebContents(event.sender);
    const { canceled, filePaths } = await dialog.showOpenDialog(win, {
      properties: ["openDirectory"],
    });
    return canceled ? null : filePaths[0];
  });

  openWindow();
  app.on("activate", () => {
    if (BrowserWindow.getAllWindows().length === 0) openWindow();
  });
});

app.on("window-all-closed", () => {
  if (process.platform !== "darwin") app.quit();
});"#, title: "js")

        Heading("Call Node from a page", id: "call-node-from-a-page").h3

        Markdown("A page has no Node in it. The *preload script* runs beside the page and hands it exactly what it may ask for: one function per channel, never `ipcRenderer` itself.")

        CodeBlock(#"// electron/preload.js
const { contextBridge, ipcRenderer } = require("electron");

const events = ["sync-progress"];

contextBridge.exposeInMainWorld("native", {
  greet: (name) => ipcRenderer.invoke("greet", name),
  startSync: () => ipcRenderer.invoke("start-sync"),
  pickFolder: () => ipcRenderer.invoke("pick-folder"),
  onEvent: (name, handler) => {
    if (!events.includes(name)) throw new Error(`No event called ${name}`);
    const listener = (_event, payload) => handler(payload);
    ipcRenderer.on(name, listener);
    return () => ipcRenderer.removeListener(name, listener);
  },
});"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("In an Electron app, one `src/native.js` takes the place of the three Tauri scripts. It keeps their names, so the `Hello`, `Sync` and `Library` pages from the Tauri sections do not change at all:")

        CodeBlock(#"// What electron/preload.js exposed, under the names the pages call.
/**
 * Whether the page is inside the app, rather than a browser tab.
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function inApp() {
  return "native" in window;
}

const outside = () => Promise.reject(new Error("This needs the desktop app."));

/**
 * @param {string} name
 * @returns {Promise<string>}
 */
function greet(name) {
  return inApp() ? window.native.greet(name) : outside();
}

/** Start syncing; how far it has got arrives as `sync-progress`. */
function startSync() {
  return inApp() ? window.native.startSync() : outside();
}

/**
 * Ask for a folder; `null` when the reader cancels.
 * @returns {Promise<?string>}
 */
function pickFolder() {
  return inApp() ? window.native.pickFolder() : Promise.resolve(null);
}

/**
 * Hear an event the native side emits, until the function it returns is called.
 * @param {string} name
 * @param {(payload: any) => void} handler
 * @returns {() => void}
 */
function onEvent(name, handler) {
  return inApp() ? window.native.onEvent(name, handler) : () => {};
}"#, title: "js")

        Heading("Run the Electron app while you work", id: "run-the-electron-app-while-you-work").h3

        Markdown("Run the dev server and the app in two terminals. In the first:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf serve")
            ])

        Markdown("In the second:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "npm start")
            ])

        Markdown("Saving a `.wf` file rebuilds and reloads the window. A change under `electron/` needs the app restarted.\n\nThe page's security policy needs nothing added: `app://localhost` is the page's own origin, as `http://localhost:3000` is while you work.")

        Heading("Package the Electron app", id: "package-the-electron-app").h3

        Markdown("Electron Forge and electron-builder both package an app from `package.json`. Whichever you use, run `wf build` first, and make sure the package holds `electron/` and `build/`; `src/` is not needed at run time. With electron-builder (`npm install --save-dev electron-builder`), the `dist` script does both:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
    "main": "electron/main.js",
    "scripts": {
        "start": "electron .",
        "dist": "wf build && electron-builder"
    },
    "build": { "files": ["electron/**", "build/**"] }
}"#, title: "json")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("What to check").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every native call fails with `This needs the desktop app.`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The page is open in a browser tab, not the app — or, with Tauri, `withGlobalTauri` is off, so `window.__TAURI__` is missing") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A Tauri call is blocked by the security policy")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`meta.connect` names both `ipc:` and `http://ipc.localhost`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A Tauri plugin's call fails")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Its permission is not granted in `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`cargo tauri build` refuses to run") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`identifier` in `tauri.conf.json` is still `com.tauri.dev`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The Tauri window shows nothing while you work")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`devUrl` does not match `dev.port`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A packaged Electron app shows an empty window")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The package is missing `build/`, or `wf build` did not run before packaging") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The app's text falls back to another font offline")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The font comes from `meta.fonts`; put the file in `public/` and declare it with `@font-face`") }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
